1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:03,098
Sweetheart, when you had that ear infection

2
00:00:03,098 --> 00:00:05,691
last month, your mother drove you to

3
00:00:05,691 --> 00:00:06,844
the urgent care,

4
00:00:06,844 --> 00:00:08,717
and they looked it up on a

5
00:00:08,717 --> 00:00:11,527
computer, and they gave you exactly the

6
00:00:11,527 --> 00:00:12,608
right medicine.

7
00:00:12,788 --> 00:00:15,328
I sat in the waiting room thinking,

8
00:00:15,328 --> 00:00:17,722
if only my mother could see this.

9
00:00:17,902 --> 00:00:20,100
Because when I was a little girl

10
00:00:20,100 --> 00:00:21,749
in the nineteen fifties,

11
00:00:21,749 --> 00:00:24,497
sweetheart, some of what the doctors did

12
00:00:24,497 --> 00:00:26,970
to us would get them arrested today.

13
00:00:27,320 --> 00:00:29,827
I'm not saying they were bad men.

14
00:00:30,007 --> 00:00:31,736
Most of them were kind,

15
00:00:31,736 --> 00:00:34,442
and they worked themselves to death.

16
00:00:34,622 --> 00:00:36,918
They just didn't know what we know

17
00:00:36,918 --> 00:00:37,188
now.

18
00:00:37,368 --> 00:00:39,545
And some things they did know,

19
00:00:39,545 --> 00:00:40,633
and did anyway,

20
00:00:40,633 --> 00:00:42,883
because that's how it was done.

21
00:00:43,063 --> 00:00:44,747
So let me tell you,

22
00:00:44,747 --> 00:00:45,721
one by one.

23
00:00:45,901 --> 00:00:48,618
Some of these will make you laugh.

24
00:00:48,798 --> 00:00:50,424
A few of them won't.

25
00:00:50,774 --> 00:00:52,039
Number one.

26
00:00:52,219 --> 00:00:54,408
Our family doctor,

27
00:00:54,408 --> 00:00:54,773
Dr.

28
00:00:54,953 --> 00:00:57,937
Hartman, smoked in the examining room.

29
00:00:58,117 --> 00:01:00,228
With me sitting on the table in

30
00:01:00,228 --> 00:01:01,182
my undershirt.

31
00:01:01,362 --> 00:01:03,593
He'd listen to my chest with his

32
00:01:03,593 --> 00:01:06,173
stethoscope and a Lucky Strike in the

33
00:01:06,173 --> 00:01:07,567
corner of his mouth,

34
00:01:07,567 --> 00:01:09,798
and the ash would get longer and

35
00:01:09,798 --> 00:01:12,239
longer, and I would just watch that

36
00:01:12,239 --> 00:01:14,330
ash, waiting for it to fall on

37
00:01:14,330 --> 00:01:14,539
me.

38
00:01:14,719 --> 00:01:16,043
It never did.

39
00:01:16,223 --> 00:01:17,570
He was an expert.

40
00:01:17,920 --> 00:01:20,480
And you know why nobody thought that

41
00:01:20,480 --> 00:01:21,333
was strange?

42
00:01:21,513 --> 00:01:24,592
Because the cigarette companies put doctors in

43
00:01:24,592 --> 00:01:25,797
their advertising.

44
00:01:25,977 --> 00:01:29,021
More doctors smoke Camels than any other

45
00:01:29,021 --> 00:01:30,467
cigarette, it said,

46
00:01:30,467 --> 00:01:32,827
in every magazine in our house.

47
00:01:33,007 --> 00:01:34,886
There was a man in a white

48
00:01:34,886 --> 00:01:37,198
coat, smiling, with a cigarette.

49
00:01:37,378 --> 00:01:40,181
My father switched to Camels because of

50
00:01:40,181 --> 00:01:40,757
that ad.

51
00:01:40,937 --> 00:01:43,584
He thought it was the healthy choice.

52
00:01:43,934 --> 00:01:47,420
The Surgeon General didn't say smoking causes

53
00:01:47,420 --> 00:01:50,830
cancer until January of nineteen sixty-four.

54
00:01:51,010 --> 00:01:53,456
By then my father had been smoking

55
00:01:53,456 --> 00:01:55,039
for twenty-five years.

56
00:01:55,219 --> 00:01:57,629
In our house there was an ashtray

57
00:01:57,629 --> 00:01:58,652
in every room,

58
00:01:58,652 --> 00:01:59,967
even the bathroom.

59
00:02:00,147 --> 00:02:02,165
And when I had a cough as

60
00:02:02,165 --> 00:02:03,295
a little girl,

61
00:02:03,295 --> 00:02:06,525
sweetheart, nobody told my father to put

62
00:02:06,525 --> 00:02:07,090
it out.

63
00:02:07,270 --> 00:02:09,633
They just told me to drink more

64
00:02:09,633 --> 00:02:10,091
water.

65
00:02:10,440 --> 00:02:13,501
Number two, and this one's true,

66
00:02:13,501 --> 00:02:14,458
I promise.

67
00:02:14,638 --> 00:02:16,677
When you went to buy shoes at

68
00:02:16,677 --> 00:02:18,366
the shoe store downtown,

69
00:02:18,366 --> 00:02:20,476
they had a big wooden machine,

70
00:02:20,476 --> 00:02:21,531
like a cabinet,

71
00:02:21,531 --> 00:02:23,008
called a fluoroscope.

72
00:02:23,188 --> 00:02:24,887
You stood on a step and put

73
00:02:24,887 --> 00:02:26,522
your feet in a slot at the

74
00:02:26,522 --> 00:02:28,976
bottom, and you looked through a little

75
00:02:28,976 --> 00:02:30,108
window at the top.

76
00:02:30,288 --> 00:02:32,339
And you could see the bones of

77
00:02:32,339 --> 00:02:33,706
your toes, wiggling,

78
00:02:33,706 --> 00:02:35,211
inside your new shoes.

79
00:02:35,391 --> 00:02:37,445
It was an X-ray machine.

80
00:02:37,796 --> 00:02:39,827
There was no lead apron.

81
00:02:40,007 --> 00:02:41,598
Nobody timed it.

82
00:02:41,778 --> 00:02:44,140
Danny and I would stand there for

83
00:02:44,140 --> 00:02:45,428
minutes at a time,

84
00:02:45,428 --> 00:02:47,647
wiggling our toes and giggling,

85
00:02:47,647 --> 00:02:50,439
while my mother talked to the salesman.

86
00:02:50,619 --> 00:02:53,418
Some children went back every week just

87
00:02:53,418 --> 00:02:54,566
to play with it.

88
00:02:54,746 --> 00:02:57,793
There were thousands of those machines in

89
00:02:57,793 --> 00:02:59,948
shoe stores all over America.

90
00:03:00,128 --> 00:03:02,036
By the end of the fifties,

91
00:03:02,036 --> 00:03:04,092
states started banning them,

92
00:03:04,092 --> 00:03:04,899
one by one.

93
00:03:05,079 --> 00:03:06,681
Now you know why.

94
00:03:07,032 --> 00:03:08,239
Number three.

95
00:03:08,419 --> 00:03:10,050
When Danny was teething,

96
00:03:10,050 --> 00:03:12,767
my mother rubbed his gums with something

97
00:03:12,767 --> 00:03:13,922
called paregoric.

98
00:03:14,102 --> 00:03:16,191
It came from the drugstore in a

99
00:03:16,191 --> 00:03:17,539
little brown bottle.

100
00:03:17,719 --> 00:03:20,119
You know what paregoric was,

101
00:03:20,119 --> 00:03:21,062
sweetheart?

102
00:03:21,242 --> 00:03:23,599
It was made with opium.

103
00:03:23,779 --> 00:03:25,846
Opium, for babies.

104
00:03:26,026 --> 00:03:27,158
And it worked,

105
00:03:27,158 --> 00:03:28,290
oh, it worked.

106
00:03:28,470 --> 00:03:31,581
Danny would fall asleep in two minutes,

107
00:03:31,581 --> 00:03:32,220
smiling.

108
00:03:32,570 --> 00:03:33,913
And for a cough,

109
00:03:33,913 --> 00:03:37,187
Grandma Pearl swore by cough syrup with

110
00:03:37,187 --> 00:03:38,363
codeine in it.

111
00:03:38,543 --> 00:03:40,357
In Ohio, in those years,

112
00:03:40,357 --> 00:03:43,306
you could buy some codeine cough syrups

113
00:03:43,306 --> 00:03:45,044
without a prescription.

114
00:03:45,224 --> 00:03:47,120
You just signed a book at the

115
00:03:47,120 --> 00:03:48,231
pharmacy counter.

116
00:03:48,411 --> 00:03:51,744
My mother signed that book every winter.

117
00:03:51,924 --> 00:03:54,640
Nobody thought of it as a drug.

118
00:03:54,820 --> 00:03:56,627
It was just what you had in

119
00:03:56,627 --> 00:03:58,768
the cupboard, next to the Vicks.

120
00:03:59,118 --> 00:04:00,551
For a scraped knee,

121
00:04:00,551 --> 00:04:02,136
it was Mercurochrome,

122
00:04:02,136 --> 00:04:04,928
that bright red stuff that stung like

123
00:04:04,928 --> 00:04:07,343
fire and stained your skin for a

124
00:04:07,343 --> 00:04:07,720
week.

125
00:04:07,900 --> 00:04:10,525
Every child in America had red knees

126
00:04:10,525 --> 00:04:11,546
in the summer.

127
00:04:11,726 --> 00:04:13,874
It was made with mercury.

128
00:04:14,054 --> 00:04:17,400
Years and years later the government decided

129
00:04:17,400 --> 00:04:19,302
it wasn't safe after all.

130
00:04:19,482 --> 00:04:22,407
I've still got a little bottle somewhere.

131
00:04:22,587 --> 00:04:23,969
I'll show you.

132
00:04:24,149 --> 00:04:25,182
Don't touch it.

133
00:04:25,533 --> 00:04:26,635
Number four.

134
00:04:26,815 --> 00:04:28,987
This is the one that scares me

135
00:04:28,987 --> 00:04:29,277
now.

136
00:04:29,457 --> 00:04:30,499
In the summer,

137
00:04:30,499 --> 00:04:32,212
to kill the mosquitoes,

138
00:04:32,212 --> 00:04:34,520
a truck came down our street at

139
00:04:34,520 --> 00:04:37,201
dusk, spraying a big white cloud out

140
00:04:37,201 --> 00:04:38,095
of the back.

141
00:04:38,275 --> 00:04:39,668
A fogging truck.

142
00:04:39,848 --> 00:04:41,450
It was DDT.

143
00:04:41,630 --> 00:04:43,704
And do you know what we children

144
00:04:43,704 --> 00:04:43,964
did?

145
00:04:44,144 --> 00:04:45,653
We ran behind it.

146
00:04:45,833 --> 00:04:47,660
Every kid on Elm Street,

147
00:04:47,660 --> 00:04:48,878
on our bicycles,

148
00:04:48,878 --> 00:04:50,857
riding right into the fog,

149
00:04:50,857 --> 00:04:53,445
because it was cool and it smelled

150
00:04:53,445 --> 00:04:53,902
sweet.

151
00:04:54,252 --> 00:04:56,806
Our mothers didn't call us in.

152
00:04:56,986 --> 00:04:59,226
They thought it was good for us.

153
00:04:59,406 --> 00:05:02,100
No mosquitoes, no disease.

154
00:05:02,280 --> 00:05:05,675
They sprayed DDT on beaches with children

155
00:05:05,675 --> 00:05:07,083
playing, on cows,

156
00:05:07,083 --> 00:05:08,491
on kitchen walls.

157
00:05:08,671 --> 00:05:11,770
There were advertisements with a happy housewife

158
00:05:11,770 --> 00:05:12,932
and a cartoon bug.

159
00:05:13,112 --> 00:05:15,968
Then in nineteen sixty-two a woman named

160
00:05:15,968 --> 00:05:18,824
Rachel Carson wrote a book called Silent

161
00:05:18,824 --> 00:05:19,323
Spring.

162
00:05:19,503 --> 00:05:21,247
And ten years later,

163
00:05:21,247 --> 00:05:23,339
in nineteen seventy-two,

164
00:05:23,339 --> 00:05:24,647
DDT was banned.

165
00:05:24,997 --> 00:05:26,181
Number five.

166
00:05:26,361 --> 00:05:27,475
Having a baby.

167
00:05:27,655 --> 00:05:30,145
When my mother had Danny in nineteen

168
00:05:30,145 --> 00:05:33,189
forty-seven, my father wasn't allowed in the

169
00:05:33,189 --> 00:05:34,157
delivery room.

170
00:05:34,337 --> 00:05:35,939
Fathers never were.

171
00:05:36,119 --> 00:05:38,406
They sat in a waiting room down

172
00:05:38,406 --> 00:05:39,734
the hall, smoking,

173
00:05:39,734 --> 00:05:41,430
with the other fathers,

174
00:05:41,430 --> 00:05:42,168
for hours.

175
00:05:42,348 --> 00:05:44,808
A nurse came out and said,

176
00:05:44,808 --> 00:05:45,849
it's a boy,

177
00:05:45,849 --> 00:05:46,133
Mr.

178
00:05:46,313 --> 00:05:48,925
Miller, and he handed out cigars.

179
00:05:49,105 --> 00:05:50,893
That's all a father did.

180
00:05:51,242 --> 00:05:52,529
And the mothers,

181
00:05:52,529 --> 00:05:53,414
sweetheart.

182
00:05:53,594 --> 00:05:57,479
Many hospitals gave women something called twilight

183
00:05:57,479 --> 00:05:57,936
sleep.

184
00:05:58,116 --> 00:06:00,171
A shot that made you forget.

185
00:06:00,351 --> 00:06:02,913
You'd wake up hours later and they'd

186
00:06:02,913 --> 00:06:04,122
bring you a baby,

187
00:06:04,122 --> 00:06:06,399
and you didn't remember a thing.

188
00:06:06,579 --> 00:06:08,438
Some women were tied to the bed

189
00:06:08,438 --> 00:06:11,017
so they wouldn't hurt themselves while they

190
00:06:11,017 --> 00:06:11,676
were under.

191
00:06:11,856 --> 00:06:14,462
My mother told me this only once,

192
00:06:14,462 --> 00:06:16,198
when she was very old.

193
00:06:16,378 --> 00:06:18,402
She didn't want to talk about it

194
00:06:18,402 --> 00:06:18,782
again.

195
00:06:19,131 --> 00:06:21,441
Then they kept you in the hospital

196
00:06:21,441 --> 00:06:22,460
a week or more,

197
00:06:22,460 --> 00:06:23,683
flat on your back.

198
00:06:23,863 --> 00:06:26,089
And the babies were kept in a

199
00:06:26,089 --> 00:06:28,392
nursery behind a glass window,

200
00:06:28,392 --> 00:06:29,006
in rows.

201
00:06:29,186 --> 00:06:31,471
The fathers would come and look at

202
00:06:31,471 --> 00:06:33,017
them through the glass,

203
00:06:33,017 --> 00:06:35,369
and the mothers saw them every four

204
00:06:35,369 --> 00:06:36,646
hours, on schedule,

205
00:06:36,646 --> 00:06:37,452
for feeding.

206
00:06:37,632 --> 00:06:39,309
Not one minute early,

207
00:06:39,309 --> 00:06:41,545
even if the baby was crying.

208
00:06:41,725 --> 00:06:43,211
That was the rule.

209
00:06:43,560 --> 00:06:44,838
Number six.

210
00:06:45,018 --> 00:06:47,537
Every child had their tonsils out.

211
00:06:47,717 --> 00:06:49,331
Every single one.

212
00:06:49,511 --> 00:06:52,123
Sore throat twice in one winter?

213
00:06:52,303 --> 00:06:53,650
Out they come.

214
00:06:53,830 --> 00:06:55,935
I had mine out when I was

215
00:06:55,935 --> 00:06:58,125
six, at Mansfield General.

216
00:06:58,305 --> 00:07:00,578
They put a rubber mask over my

217
00:07:00,578 --> 00:07:02,775
face that smelled like ether,

218
00:07:02,775 --> 00:07:04,290
a sweet, sick smell,

219
00:07:04,290 --> 00:07:06,942
and told me to count backwards from

220
00:07:06,942 --> 00:07:07,245
ten.

221
00:07:07,425 --> 00:07:08,772
I got to seven.

222
00:07:09,121 --> 00:07:10,265
When I woke up,

223
00:07:10,265 --> 00:07:12,933
my throat felt like I'd swallowed a

224
00:07:12,933 --> 00:07:15,296
cactus, and the nurse gave me a

225
00:07:15,296 --> 00:07:16,668
bowl of ice cream.

226
00:07:16,848 --> 00:07:18,380
That was the best part.

227
00:07:18,560 --> 00:07:20,798
All the children in the ward were

228
00:07:20,798 --> 00:07:21,951
eating ice cream.

229
00:07:22,131 --> 00:07:26,340
Today, sweetheart, doctors hardly ever take tonsils

230
00:07:26,340 --> 00:07:28,980
out, unless they really have to.

231
00:07:29,160 --> 00:07:32,147
Back then, they took out millions of

232
00:07:32,147 --> 00:07:33,143
them a year.

233
00:07:33,323 --> 00:07:35,530
Mine were perfectly fine,

234
00:07:35,530 --> 00:07:37,119
I found out later.

235
00:07:37,469 --> 00:07:39,808
And here's the part that broke my

236
00:07:39,808 --> 00:07:40,233
heart.

237
00:07:40,413 --> 00:07:42,393
While I was in that hospital,

238
00:07:42,393 --> 00:07:44,647
my mother could only visit me for

239
00:07:44,647 --> 00:07:45,672
one hour a day.

240
00:07:45,852 --> 00:07:48,401
Visiting hours, they called it,

241
00:07:48,401 --> 00:07:49,881
from two to three.

242
00:07:50,061 --> 00:07:53,255
Some hospitals let parents see their children

243
00:07:53,255 --> 00:07:54,391
only on Sundays.

244
00:07:54,571 --> 00:07:57,086
They said seeing the mothers made the

245
00:07:57,086 --> 00:07:58,310
children cry more.

246
00:07:58,490 --> 00:08:00,484
So you lay there at night,

247
00:08:00,484 --> 00:08:01,558
six years old,

248
00:08:01,558 --> 00:08:03,705
in a room full of strangers,

249
00:08:03,705 --> 00:08:05,316
and you cried anyway.

250
00:08:05,666 --> 00:08:08,226
Now, I'll tell you one thing that

251
00:08:08,226 --> 00:08:09,080
was better.

252
00:08:09,260 --> 00:08:10,653
House calls.

253
00:08:10,833 --> 00:08:12,672
If you were too sick to go

254
00:08:12,672 --> 00:08:14,653
out, the doctor came to you.

255
00:08:14,833 --> 00:08:15,878
Dr.

256
00:08:16,058 --> 00:08:18,647
Hartman came up our front walk with

257
00:08:18,647 --> 00:08:20,275
his black leather bag,

258
00:08:20,275 --> 00:08:21,162
in the snow,

259
00:08:21,162 --> 00:08:23,012
at nine o'clock at night.

260
00:08:23,192 --> 00:08:24,626
He sat on my bed,

261
00:08:24,626 --> 00:08:26,059
felt my forehead,

262
00:08:26,059 --> 00:08:28,926
gave me a shot of penicillin right

263
00:08:28,926 --> 00:08:32,468
there, and charged my father five dollars.

264
00:08:32,648 --> 00:08:34,440
Then he had a cup of coffee

265
00:08:34,440 --> 00:08:35,435
in the kitchen.

266
00:08:35,784 --> 00:08:38,036
Penicillin was still new,

267
00:08:38,036 --> 00:08:38,757
you see.

268
00:08:38,937 --> 00:08:41,327
It had only been in drugstores since

269
00:08:41,327 --> 00:08:42,257
after the war.

270
00:08:42,437 --> 00:08:45,325
And doctors gave it for everything,

271
00:08:45,325 --> 00:08:47,141
a cold, a sore throat,

272
00:08:47,141 --> 00:08:48,544
whatever you had.

273
00:08:48,724 --> 00:08:50,694
A shot in the behind with a

274
00:08:50,694 --> 00:08:52,008
big glass syringe,

275
00:08:52,008 --> 00:08:54,416
which they boiled on the stove to

276
00:08:54,416 --> 00:08:54,854
clean.

277
00:08:55,034 --> 00:08:56,520
Not thrown away.

278
00:08:56,700 --> 00:08:58,567
Boiled and used again,

279
00:08:58,567 --> 00:09:00,264
on the next patient.

280
00:09:00,614 --> 00:09:03,273
And now the one that isn't funny.

281
00:09:03,453 --> 00:09:04,440
Polio.

282
00:09:04,620 --> 00:09:07,290
Every summer when I was a child,

283
00:09:07,290 --> 00:09:09,543
our mothers were terrified.

284
00:09:09,723 --> 00:09:12,715
Polio was a virus that could paralyze

285
00:09:12,715 --> 00:09:14,494
a child in a few days.

286
00:09:14,674 --> 00:09:17,681
Nobody knew exactly how it spread.

287
00:09:17,861 --> 00:09:19,712
So in July and August,

288
00:09:19,712 --> 00:09:22,320
they closed the swimming pools.

289
00:09:22,500 --> 00:09:25,379
They closed the movie theaters to children.

290
00:09:25,559 --> 00:09:28,130
My mother wouldn't let us drink from

291
00:09:28,130 --> 00:09:29,344
a water fountain.

292
00:09:29,693 --> 00:09:31,470
In nineteen fifty-two,

293
00:09:31,470 --> 00:09:32,680
the worst year,

294
00:09:32,680 --> 00:09:36,636
there were fifty-seven thousand cases in America.

295
00:09:36,816 --> 00:09:38,600
Children in iron lungs,

296
00:09:38,600 --> 00:09:41,626
big metal tubes that breathed for them,

297
00:09:41,626 --> 00:09:43,875
in long rows in the hospital.

298
00:09:44,055 --> 00:09:46,733
A boy in my second-grade class went

299
00:09:46,733 --> 00:09:49,258
in one August and never came back

300
00:09:49,258 --> 00:09:50,023
to school.

301
00:09:50,203 --> 00:09:51,851
We didn't talk about it.

302
00:09:52,031 --> 00:09:54,632
We just looked at his empty desk.

303
00:09:54,981 --> 00:09:57,180
Then on the twelfth of April,

304
00:09:57,180 --> 00:10:00,819
nineteen fifty-five, they announced on the radio

305
00:10:00,819 --> 00:10:01,425
that Dr.

306
00:10:01,605 --> 00:10:04,066
Jonas Salk's vaccine worked.

307
00:10:04,246 --> 00:10:05,825
Church bells rang.

308
00:10:06,005 --> 00:10:07,967
People cried in the street.

309
00:10:08,147 --> 00:10:10,172
Two weeks later they lined us up

310
00:10:10,172 --> 00:10:12,513
in the school gymnasium for the shot.

311
00:10:12,693 --> 00:10:15,195
I've never seen a line of children

312
00:10:15,195 --> 00:10:17,181
so happy to get a needle in

313
00:10:17,181 --> 00:10:17,917
their arm.

314
00:10:18,267 --> 00:10:20,587
But even that had a dark side,

315
00:10:20,587 --> 00:10:21,437
sweetheart.

316
00:10:21,617 --> 00:10:24,720
One company, Cutter Laboratories,

317
00:10:24,720 --> 00:10:26,319
made a bad batch.

318
00:10:26,499 --> 00:10:29,425
Some of their vaccine had live virus

319
00:10:29,425 --> 00:10:29,912
in it.

320
00:10:30,092 --> 00:10:32,729
Tens of thousands of children got sick

321
00:10:32,729 --> 00:10:34,811
from it, and some of them died

322
00:10:34,811 --> 00:10:36,060
or were paralyzed.

323
00:10:36,240 --> 00:10:39,364
After that, the government made the rules

324
00:10:39,364 --> 00:10:40,431
much stricter.

325
00:10:40,611 --> 00:10:43,320
A lot of today's rules were written

326
00:10:43,320 --> 00:10:45,719
because of a mistake like that.

327
00:10:46,069 --> 00:10:47,674
And when I was sixteen,

328
00:10:47,674 --> 00:10:49,906
my friend Carol went to a doctor

329
00:10:49,906 --> 00:10:52,488
because she thought she was too heavy

330
00:10:52,488 --> 00:10:53,813
for her prom dress.

331
00:10:53,993 --> 00:10:55,773
She wasn't, sweetheart,

332
00:10:55,773 --> 00:10:57,012
she was a stick.

333
00:10:57,192 --> 00:10:59,240
He gave her a bottle of little

334
00:10:59,240 --> 00:11:01,151
pills to take every morning.

335
00:11:01,331 --> 00:11:02,794
Diet pills.

336
00:11:02,974 --> 00:11:04,750
They were amphetamines.

337
00:11:04,930 --> 00:11:07,504
She cleaned her whole house at midnight

338
00:11:07,504 --> 00:11:09,551
and couldn't sleep for a month.

339
00:11:09,731 --> 00:11:12,568
Doctors handed those out like candy,

340
00:11:12,568 --> 00:11:14,460
to girls, to housewives,

341
00:11:14,460 --> 00:11:15,327
to anybody.

342
00:11:15,676 --> 00:11:17,650
And one last story.

343
00:11:17,830 --> 00:11:19,588
Around nineteen sixty,

344
00:11:19,588 --> 00:11:22,465
a German company had a sleeping pill

345
00:11:22,465 --> 00:11:23,983
called thalidomide.

346
00:11:24,163 --> 00:11:26,680
Doctors in Europe gave it to pregnant

347
00:11:26,680 --> 00:11:28,517
women for morning sickness.

348
00:11:28,697 --> 00:11:31,335
And an American company wanted to sell

349
00:11:31,335 --> 00:11:32,169
it here too.

350
00:11:32,349 --> 00:11:34,578
It was almost on the shelves.

351
00:11:34,758 --> 00:11:36,476
One woman stopped it.

352
00:11:36,656 --> 00:11:38,785
A doctor at the Food and Drug

353
00:11:38,785 --> 00:11:41,428
Administration named Frances Kelsey.

354
00:11:41,777 --> 00:11:44,284
She said the company hadn't proven it

355
00:11:44,284 --> 00:11:46,520
was safe, and she kept saying no,

356
00:11:46,520 --> 00:11:47,943
for more than a year,

357
00:11:47,943 --> 00:11:49,637
while they pressured her.

358
00:11:49,817 --> 00:11:52,255
And then the news came from Europe.

359
00:11:52,435 --> 00:11:55,373
Thousands of babies were being born with

360
00:11:55,373 --> 00:11:59,046
terrible problems, because their mothers took that

361
00:11:59,046 --> 00:11:59,413
pill.

362
00:11:59,593 --> 00:12:02,089
President Kennedy gave Dr.

363
00:12:02,269 --> 00:12:05,311
Kelsey a medal in nineteen sixty-two.

364
00:12:05,491 --> 00:12:08,393
American mothers like me owe her more

365
00:12:08,393 --> 00:12:09,647
than we can say.

366
00:12:09,997 --> 00:12:13,167
So, sweetheart, that's how it was.

367
00:12:13,347 --> 00:12:16,720
Smoking doctors, X-ray shoe machines,

368
00:12:16,720 --> 00:12:20,092
opium for babies and DDT for dessert.

369
00:12:20,272 --> 00:12:22,337
We survived most of it,

370
00:12:22,337 --> 00:12:24,313
but not everybody did.

371
00:12:24,493 --> 00:12:27,022
So next time you complain about all

372
00:12:27,022 --> 00:12:29,480
the warnings on a medicine bottle,

373
00:12:29,480 --> 00:12:32,371
remember, every one of those warnings is

374
00:12:32,371 --> 00:12:34,033
there because somebody,

375
00:12:34,033 --> 00:12:36,346
somewhere, learned the hard way.

376
00:12:36,696 --> 00:12:39,135
Now tell me in the comments.

377
00:12:39,315 --> 00:12:41,384
Did your shoe store have one of

378
00:12:41,384 --> 00:12:42,786
those X-ray machines?

379
00:12:42,966 --> 00:12:45,265
Did you run behind the fogging truck?

380
00:12:45,445 --> 00:12:47,378
I want to know I wasn't the

381
00:12:47,378 --> 00:12:48,022
only one.

382
00:12:48,202 --> 00:12:50,118
Goodnight, sweetheart.

383
00:12:50,298 --> 00:12:52,940
Grandma Ruth will be right here with

384
00:12:52,940 --> 00:12:54,628
another story tomorrow.
